---
/* Retitled 2026-10-23 (Justin): this page is about thermal HOG COUNTS before a
   Red Creek trapping job, nothing broader. General wildlife surveys, deer
   counts and ranch consulting belong to backcountrydrones.com, and this page
   must not compete with Backcountry's survey page for those searches. Keep
   the link to it. URL stays /wildlife-surveys (it's indexed). */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Hog Trapping Service', path: '/hog-trapping-service' },
  { name: 'Thermal Hog Counts', path: '/wildlife-surveys' },
];
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Thermal hog count before trapping',
  serviceType: 'Feral hog survey',
  provider: [
    { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#business` },
    { '@type': 'LocalBusiness', name: 'Backcountry Drones LLC', url: SITE.partner.url },
  ],
};
---

<Base
  title="Thermal Hog Counts Before Trapping | Red Creek Outdoors"
  description="Before a trap goes up, a thermal count shows how many hogs are using your land and where they bed. Flown by Backcountry Drones for Red Creek trapping jobs."
  crumbs={crumbs}
  schema={schema}
>
  <PageHero eyebrow="Hog trapping" title="Thermal Hog Counts Before We Trap" crumbs={crumbs}
    lede="Know how many hogs you're dealing with before the first trap goes up. The count is flown by our partner Backcountry Drones." />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>Why count before trapping</h2>
      <p>
        Hogs do most of their moving after dark, and a thermal camera sees warm bodies through it. A count tells us how
        many sounders are using the place and where they bed, so the traps go in the right spot the first time and we
        know when the whole group is inside instead of guessing.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also gives you a starting number. When the trapping is done, you know what came off the land against what was
        on it.
      </p>

      <h2>How the work splits</h2>
      <table class="spec">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Backcountry Drones</th><td>Flies the thermal count and delivers the results. Also builds the remote trap releases and runs the field cameras on our trap line</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Red Creek Outdoors</th><td>Reads the count with you, sets the traps and works the property until the sounder is gone</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Counts and hunts stay separate.</strong> Texas Parks and Wildlife says using a drone to hunt, drive,
          capture, take, count or photograph wildlife is unlawful without a TPWD permit, and drones can't be used to find
          wounded game. Backcountry Drones flies under its TPWD Aerial Wildlife Management permit. Nobody flies a drone
          during one of our hunts.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Full wildlife surveys</h2>
      <p>
        Deer counts, full-ranch surveys, timing, deliverables and pricing are Backcountry Drones' work, and they're on
        their site: <a href="https://backcountrydrones.com/services/wildlife-surveys">thermal drone wildlife surveys</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ready to get hogs off your land? Start with a <a href="/hog-trap-request">hog trap request</a> or read{' '}
        <a href="/hog-trapping-service">how our trapping works</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="sources">Source: <a href="https://tpwd.texas.gov/warden/law-enforcement-faq/drones-uavs">TPWD Law Enforcement FAQ, Drones</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand title="Hogs on your place?" text="Tell us the county and acreage and what the hogs are tearing up. We'll set up the site assessment." />
</Base>
